A1. This is always going around. ~ EDDY
A4. This woman lost her husband—but will probably find another. ~ WIDOW
A8. First name of famous painter of horses. ~ ROSA
A12. You may be subject to this, but not I. ~ ARE
A13. A fast stepper. ~ RACER
A14. Uncle Tom's earthly home, ~ CABIN
A15. Partings that bring embarrassment. ~ RIPS
A17. Nevertheless. ~ YET
A18. The politician that wins. ~ SLIEST
A19. Reclines—as on a lamp-post. ~ LEANS
A21. This is your fate. ~ DESTINY
A23. What to do with your sorrows—if you can. ~ DROWN
A25. Paper comes in these. ~ REAMS
A28. Done to many a case—either law or Scotch. ~ TRIED
A30. What tides do instead of waiting. ~ EBB
A31. Even the Scotch comes across on this. ~ BAR
A33. This denotes presents. ~ AT
A35. Values highly. ~ ESTEEMS
A37. One who talks instead of listening to us. ~ BORE
A38. Approaches. ~ NEARS
A40. Prefix meaning quarter dozen. ~ TRI
A41. Seeps. ~ OOZES
A42. These work far into the night. ~ JAWS
A43. What many a jest is spoken in. ~ EARNEST
A45. In this or that manner. ~ SO
A46. Gold-diggers dig for this. ~ ORE
A47. This is the cat's—if she is quick enough. ~ RAT
A48. Horses have these. ~ GAITS
A50. This has a well-known bark. ~ BIRCH
A52. What Moses probably did to the candle when the light went out. ~ RELIT
A55. Not many of these hang around chorus girls nowadays. ~ CLOTHES
A57. A kind of race. ~ RELAY
A59. This is never rough. ~ SMOOTH
A61. To make free. ~ RID
A63. To get his goat. ~ RILE
A64. These have two legs—but neither arms nor wings. ~ PANTS
A65. Women follow these wherever they go. ~ MODES
A66. A milk manufacturer. ~ COW
A67. What the T. B. M. does at the theater. ~ SEES
A68. Dew will do this to a rose. ~ BEDEW
A69. What the boss does to the boy whose grandmother dies more than once a month. ~ CANS

D1. Title of nobility. ~ EARL
D2. Dehydrated, as herrings and a few bars. ~ DRIED
D3. What a snuggle-puppy hates to do. ~ DEPART
D4. A track. ~ WAY
D5. A kind of cold drink. ~ ICED
D6. Prohibition is supposed to do this. ~ DETER
D7. "Either's" little brother. ~ OR
D8. These are all wet. ~ RAINS
D9. Omitted from many marriage ceremonies—and all marriages. ~ OBEY
D10. What Judge, Jr., calls Judgette. ~ SIS
D11. Busy as bees. ~ ANTS
D14. Do this and you'll get up in the world. ~ CLIMB
D16. Heard in many a Pullman. ~ SNORERS
D18. These are sticking propositions. ~ STABS
D20. A kind of cheese. ~ SWISS
D22. Appearing. ~ SEEMING
D24. Many a poor fish get caught in this. ~ NET
D26. Hideous night quadrupeds. ~ MARES
D27. Pick on this and there'll be music in the air. ~ BANJO
D29. Do this—and there'll be a separation. ~ DETATCH
D31. Dickens. ~ BOZ
D32. Do this to make up for the wearin' of the green. ~ RESOD
D34. Feminine persuaders. ~ TEARS
D36. It's wrong to do this. ~ ERR
D37. One who puts liquid into glass containers. ~ BOTTLER
D39. What married men are inspired with. ~ AWE
D41. A willowy plant. ~ OSIER
D43. This is all-the-world to most people. ~ EARTH
D44. Good for listening-in. ~ EAR
D47. Social affairs where the bricks are not ice cream. ~ RIOTS
D49. This comes in quartz. ~ SILICA
D50. These spot many a character. ~ BLOTS
D51. An historical character who was death on children. ~ HEROD
D53. Claw. ~ TALON
D54. Cleopatra used one of these as a ticket to another world. (Plural.) ~ ASPS
D55. Ice cream receptacle. ~ CONE
D56. To engage in a faction. ~ SIDE
D58. Bows were made with these. ~ YEWS
D60. A girl's name. ~ MAE
D62. This is all wet. ~ DEW
D65. I—myself. ~ ME


